008817784012499,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(x(x+10) = 10(-10-x)\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Expand both sides of the equation.\n2. Simplify and combine like terms.\n3. Solve the resulting quadratic equation.\n\nLet's start with the given equation:\n\\[x(x+10) = 10(-10-x)\\]\n\nFirst, expand both sides:\n\\[x^2 + 10x = 10(-10-x)\\]\n\\[x^2 + 10x = -100 - 10x\\]\n\nNext, we move all terms to one side of the equation to set it to zero:\n\\[x^2 + 10x + 10x + 100 = 0\\]\n\\[x^2 + 20x + 100 = 0\\]\n\nNow, we recognize that the left side of the equation is a perfect square:\n\\[(x + 10)^2 = 0\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we take the square root of both sides:\n\\[x + 10 = 0\\]\n\nSubtract 10 from both sides:\n\\[x = -10\\]\n\nThus, the only solution to the equation is:\n\\[\\boxed{-10}\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-10}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:566"}
